  "text": "We are given the equation:\n\n$$\n\\lceil x^3 \\rceil - \\lceil x \\rceil^3 = 42\n$$\n\nWe are to find the **largest solution** to this equation.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the notation\n\n- $\\lceil x \\rceil$ is the **ceiling function**, which gives the **least integer greater than or equal to** $x$.\n- So, for example, $\\lceil 1.2 \\rceil = 2$, and $\\lceil 1.999 \\rceil = 2$.\n\nLet’s denote:\n\n- Let $n = \\lceil x \\rceil$\n- Then $x \\in [n - 1, n)$\n\nSo, the equation becomes:\n\n$$\n\\lceil x^3 \\rceil - n^3 = 42\n\\Rightarrow \\lceil x^3 \\rceil = n^3 + 42\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Try to find integers $n$ such that $n^3 + 42$ is an integer (since the ceiling function of $x^3$ is also an integer)\n\nSo $n^3 + 42$ must be an integer. That means $n^3$ must be an integer.\n\nSo, let’s try small integer values of $n$ and compute $n^3 + 42$ to see if it is possible for $\\lceil x^3 \\rceil = n^3 + 42$.\n\nLet’s try $n = 3$:\n\n- $n^3 = 27$\n- $n^3 + 42 = 69$\n- So $\\lceil x^3 \\rceil = 69$\n\nThen $x \\in [3, 4)$, and we want:\n\n$$\nx^3 \\in [27, 69)\n\\Rightarrow x \\in [3, \\sqrt[3]{69})\n\\approx [3, 4.09)\n$$\n\nNow check if there exists an $x$ in $[3, 4.09)$ such that $\\lceil x^3 \\rceil = 69$. Since $x^3$ is in $[27, 69)$, $\\lceil x^3 \\rceil$ would be 69 if and only if $x^3$ is in the interval $[69, 69)$, but $x^3 < 69$, so this is **not** possible.\n\nTry $n = 4$:\n\n- $n^3 = 64$\n- $n^3 + 42 = 106$\n- So $\\lceil x^3 \\rceil = 106$\n\nThen $x \\in [4, 5)$, and we want:\n\n$$\nx^3 \\in [64, 106)\n\\Rightarrow x \\in [4, \\sqrt[3]{106}) \\approx [4, 4.72)\n$$\n\nNow check if there exists an $x$ in $[4, 4.72)$ such that $\\lceil x^3 \\rceil = 106$. Since $x^3$ is in $[64, 106)$, $\\lceil x^3 \\rceil$ would be 106 if and only if $x^3$ is in $[106, 107)$, which is not the case.\n\nTry $n = 5$:\n\n- $n^3 = 125$\n- $n^3 + 42 = 167$\n- So $\\lceil x^3 \\rceil = 167$\n\nThen $x \\in [5, 6)$, and we want:\n\n$$\nx^3 \\in [125, 167)\n\\Right